        "description": "<p><span>\n\nWhen I was reading about the history of contra dance for my </span>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.jefftk.com/final-papers/thesis/contra-thesis-1-0-0.pdf\">college\nthesis</a> I learned that Jack Perron (who wrote the \n\n<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/England-Fiddlers-Repertoire-Miller-Perron/dp/0977053067/\">New\nEngland Fiddler's Repertoire</a> with Randy Miller) used to play for\ndances with a \n\n<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stroh_violin\">Stroh violin</a>.\nThis is a violin that uses a membrane and horn for amplification,\ninstead of a resonating wooden body, which is more directional and\n(unfortunately not much) louder.  There's a \n\n<a href=\"https://www.discogs.com/release/4769017-Caitriona-Yeats-Jack-Perron-Randy-Miller-Irish-Tunes-On-Harp-Stroh-Violin\">recording</a>\nbut I haven't been able to find a copy.\n\n\n\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.jefftk.com/jack-perron-and-his-stroh-violin-big.jpg\"><img src=\"https://www.jefftk.com/jack-perron-and-his-stroh-violin.jpg\" width=\"550\" height=",
